[INFO] 09-22 05:35:25 execution.py:372] **Reasoning:**  
Each sparse set \( S_i \) has at most a polynomial number \( p_i(n) \) of strings of length \( n \). To combine \( k \) sparse sets \( S_1, S_2, \ldots, S_k \) into a single sparse set \( S \), define \( S \) as  
\[ S = \{ \langle x, i \rangle \mid x \in S_i \text{ and } 1 \leq i \leq k \} \]  
This formation ensures \( S \) is sparse because the number of strings \(\langle x, i \rangle\) of length \( n + \log_2 k \) is bounded by \( \sum_{i=1}^{k} p_i(n) \), a polynomial sum.

**Answer:**  
Define \( S = \{ \langle x, i \rangle \mid x \in S_i, 1 \leq i \leq k \} \); \( S \) is a sparse set.
