\[ \frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} g(\nu, x) = -\frac{1}{h(\nu, x)^2} \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} h(\nu, x). \]  
To find \(\frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} h(\nu, x)\), apply the chain rule to \( h(\nu, x) = \sqrt{(x^2-1)(1-\nu^2x^2)} \), giving  
\[ \frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} h(\nu, x) = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{(x^2-1)(1-\nu^2x^2)}} \cdot \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} \left((x^2-1)(1-\nu^2x^2)\right) \right). \]  
The derivative of the internal function is  
\[ (x^2-1) \cdot (-2\nu x^2). \]  
Thus, combine the results:  
\[ \frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} h(\nu, x) = \frac{-(x^2-1)\nu x^2}{\sqrt{(x^2-1)(1-\nu^2x^2)}}. \]  
Therefore,  
\[ \frac{\partial}{\partial \nu} g(\nu, x) = \frac{(x^2-1)\nu x^2}{((x^2-1)(1-\nu^2x^2))^\frac{3}{2}}. \]

